* sg13cmos5l-bandgap -- closed-loop vref bench body (issue #14, Part of #5).
* Template: sim/grid-run.sh substitutes @@VDD@@ and @@WORK@@ and writes the deck to
* sim/build/closed-loop-vref-pvt/deck.cir. Model `.lib` cards, temperature and
* (for the 45-point request) the supply value come from the `klt sim` request.
*
* This is also the shared DC-supply fixture: closed-loop-iq and
* closed-loop-vref-pvt-boxtc name this file in their bench.json because their
* supply stimulus is byte-identical to this one -- see sim/README.md, "Deck
* fixture conventions". What a bench runs is whatever deck file its bench.json
* names -- fixture by filename, not fixture by directory.
*
* This bench's own fixture note: the loop starts from the solver's own operating
* point and settles during the transient; the startup cell in the DUT is what keeps
* it off the zero state. This bench's measured story is in README.md.
*
* The five explanations shared by every grid deck fixture (NO SPICE TITLE LINE,
* rshunt/gmin as convergence aids, the `.global sub!` substrate tie, the `Xdut`
* staged-DUT wrapper, osdi_preload instead of a .control block) are stated once in
* sim/README.md under "Deck fixture conventions", not repeated per template.

.options rshunt=1e9 gmin=1e-9

Vvdd vdd 0 dc @@VDD@@
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
.global sub!
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

.include "@@WORK@@/bandgap_top.spice"
Xdut vdd vref vss sg13cmos5l_bandgap_top
